 "title": "The resting membrane potential",
 "group": "Membranes",
 "order": 6,
 "mode": "electrical",
 "description": "The parallel-conductance model of a membrane. Each kind of ion channel is a conductance (drawn as a resistor: conductance = 1 / resistance) in series with a battery at that ion’s equilibrium (Nernst) potential: K⁺ at −90 mV and Na⁺ at +60 mV. At rest the membrane settles at the average of these potentials, weighted by the conductances: <i>V</i> = (<i>g</i><sub>K</sub><i>E</i><sub>K</sub> + <i>g</i><sub>Na</sub><i>E</i><sub>Na</sub>) / (<i>g</i><sub>K</sub> + <i>g</i><sub>Na</sub>), about −70 mV here – close to <i>E</i><sub>K</sub>, because the K⁺ conductance is much larger. Run it, then close the switch to open many more Na⁺ channels and watch the membrane potential swing towards <i>E</i><sub>Na</sub>.",
